<h1>Matrix and Cases</h1>
<p><strong>Matrix and Cases</strong> generates one test case for every combination of values declared by the
	test's matrix. It follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/">Lambda Configuration
	Parameters</a>, which introduces callback-driven configuration choices in the same tutorial suite. Select the
	test to see the generated cases below its regular configuration fields:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/matrix-and-cases-config.png", "Matrix and Cases test selected in the tutorial suite, showing 24 selected cases generated from algorithm, seed, height and width dimensions", "large") }}
<p>The summary reports <strong>Total Cases: 24</strong>, and the table shows how each case was assembled. The
	<strong>algorithm</strong>, <strong>seed</strong>, <strong>height</strong> and <strong>width</strong> columns come
	directly from the matrix dimensions. <strong>Case</strong> is the generated case number, while
	<strong>Order</strong> is the position in which that case will run. With <strong>Run Order</strong> set to
	<strong>In Order</strong>, those values are the same.</p>
<p>Each check mark in <strong>Select</strong> includes that case in the run. Clearing one omits only that
	combination; it does not change the matrix definition. <strong>Case Cycles</strong> controls how many times
	each selected case runs, independently of the test-level <strong>Cycles</strong> value.</p>

<h2 id="defining-the-matrix">Defining the matrix</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_matrix_and_cases/") }}
<p>Declare a <code>Matrix</code> field on the test config and add one <code>Dimension</code> for each case
	parameter. The first argument is the key used in the generated case; the second is the set of values that
	dimension can take:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("algorithm", ("BFS", "DFS", "ASTAR")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("seed", (42, 100)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("height", (5, 10)),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension("width", (15, 20)),
)') }}
<p>Orcraft builds the Cartesian product of those values. This matrix therefore creates
	<code>3 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 24</code> cases. In the default order, the last dimension changes fastest: width
	alternates first, followed by height, seed and then algorithm, which is the sequence visible in the table.</p>

<h2 id="using-matrix-values">Using matrix values</h2>
<p>Orcraft creates an <code>OrcraftTestCase</code> for each row in the Cartesian product, stores that row's
	dimension choices in the case's <code>params</code> mapping, and passes the case to the test's
	<code>_run_case</code> method.</p>
<p>For example, the first row in the screenshot produces a mapping equivalent to:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('{
	"algorithm": "BFS",
	"seed": 42,
	"height": 5,
	"width": 15,
}') }}
<p>The complete test class receives that generated case and uses its values to configure the Maze application:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('class TestMatrixAndCases(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: TestMatrixAndCasesConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or TestMatrixAndCasesConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

		maze_app = MazeApp(
			self.config.maze_executable_folder,
			"solve",
			width=case.params["width"],
			height=case.params["height"],
			seed=case.params["seed"],
			start_row=0,
			start_col=0,
			end_row=case.params["height"] - 1,
			end_col=case.params["width"] - 1,
			algorithm=case.params["algorithm"],
		)

		maze_app.run()

		return case.result') }}
<p>The <code>case: OrcraftTestCase</code> parameter is the framework interface for each generated combination.
	Every key used with <code>case.params</code> matches a dimension key in the matrix declaration above. Orcraft
	calls <code>_run_case</code> once for each selected combination, using the configured order and cycle counts.</p>

<h2 id="running-generated-cases">Running the generated cases</h2>
<p>With all 24 cases selected and <strong>Case Cycles</strong> set to <code>1</code>, click
	<strong class="text-purple">Run</strong>, then click <strong>Yes</strong> to confirm. Orcraft executes each
	matrix combination once. The test row reports 24 planned, completed, and passed items, while the log presents
	one result row for every generated case:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/running-matrix-and-cases-test.png", "Completed Matrix and Cases run with all 24 generated cases passing and the log listing each case result", "large") }}
<p>The result table preserves the matrix context for every execution. Its <strong>algorithm</strong>,
	<strong>seed</strong>, <strong>height</strong>, and <strong>width</strong> columns identify the exact combination
	used by each case, so a failure can be traced to its inputs without reconstructing the matrix order.</p>

<h3 id="inspecting-case-results">Inspecting case results</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed test run to inspect the full execution hierarchy. The left
	panel compares default, planned, and run configuration, including the matrix dimensions and the cases included
	in the plan. The results tree on the right contains one node for each case and one
	<strong>Case Cycle 1</strong> child beneath it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/checking-cases-statuses-in-details.png", "Matrix and Cases Details view showing 24 passing case nodes, each with one case-cycle result", "large") }}
<p>The summary distinguishes generated cases from the surrounding result nodes. In this run, all 24 planned
	cases completed and passed. Expanding a case exposes its status, timing, and the cycle execution associated
	with that matrix combination.</p>

<h2 id="repeating-each-case">Repeating each case</h2>
<p><strong>Case Cycles</strong> controls repetition within each generated case. Change it from <code>1</code> to
	<code>2</code> while all 24 cases are selected. <strong>Total Cases</strong> immediately changes from
	<code>24</code> to <code>48</code>, reflecting 24 selected cases multiplied by two case cycles:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/changing-case-cycles.png", "Matrix and Cases configuration with Case Cycles changed to two and Total Cases increased from 24 to 48", "large") }}
<p>This setting is independent of the test-level <strong>Cycles</strong> value. Test cycles repeat the complete
	test, while case cycles repeat each selected case within a test cycle.</p>

<h3 id="planning-and-running-case-cycles">Planning and running case cycles</h3>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> to commit the new case-cycle count. The
	<strong>Case Cycles</strong> value and <strong>Total Cases: 48</strong> indicator turn blue. The previous run is
	marked as a plan mismatch because it executed each case only once:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/updating-plan-with-case-cycles.png", "Matrix and Cases plan updated to two case cycles and 48 total case executions", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm the run. The completed test reports 48 planned,
	completed, and passed executions. The log records each repetition independently; the example below shows the
	configuration, Maze output, and passing result for <strong>Case Cycle 2</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/running-with-two-case-cycles.png", "Completed Matrix and Cases run with 48 passing executions and the log showing the configuration, output, and result for Case Cycle 2", "large") }}
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed test run. Every generated case contains two children,
	<strong>Case Cycle 1</strong> and <strong>Case Cycle 2</strong>, with independent status and timing:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/checking-each-case-has-two-cycles-in-details.png", "Matrix and Cases Details view showing two passing case-cycle results beneath each generated case and 48 completed executions", "large") }}
<p>The hierarchy makes the multiplication visible: the matrix defines the cases, case selection determines which
	combinations participate, and <strong>Case Cycles</strong> determines how many times each selected combination
	runs.</p>

<h3 id="removing-a-run">Removing a run</h3>
<p>This removal is not required for the Matrix and Cases workflow; it is included to demonstrate how Orcraft
	removes an individual run instance. The execution tree retains a separate row for every run. Before returning
	the configuration to one case cycle, remove the last two-cycle run used in the preceding example. Right-click
	that run and click <strong class="text-danger">Remove</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/removing-last-run.png", "Remove action opened for the last Matrix and Cases run while another run instance remains in the execution tree", "large") }}
<p>After removal, the earlier one-cycle run remains and is marked with
	<strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong> because the current configuration still plans two case
	cycles and 48 executions. Orcraft excludes plan-mismatched runs from the current plan's aggregate statistics.
	Because the removed two-cycle run was the only run that matched the plan, the test now shows
	<strong>Done: 0</strong>, <strong>Pass: 0</strong>, <strong>Fail: 0</strong>, <strong>Progress: 0%</strong>, and
	<strong>Pass %: 0%</strong>, even though the retained one-cycle run completed successfully. The Matrix and Cases
	test and its 48-execution configuration are unchanged:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/after-rmoving-run.png", "Matrix and Cases after removing the last two-cycle run, with the earlier one-cycle run retained as a plan mismatch and the 48-execution configuration unchanged", "large") }}
<p><strong>Remove</strong> deletes only the selected run instance and its recorded results.</p>

<h3 id="restoring-one-case-cycle">Restoring one case cycle</h3>
<p>Set <strong>Case Cycles</strong> from <code>2</code> back to <code>1</code>. This reduces
	<strong>Total Cases</strong> from <code>48</code> to <code>24</code> without changing the selected cases or the
	<strong>Maze Executable Folder</strong>. Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> to commit the
	one-cycle configuration:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/reset-to-defaults-and-updating-plan-again.png", "Matrix and Cases configuration returned to one case cycle and 24 total executions while retaining the configured Maze Executable Folder", "large") }}

<h2 id="selecting-cases">Selecting a subset of cases</h2>

<h3 id="clearing-the-case-selection">Clearing the case selection</h3>
<p>The matrix continues to define 24 possible combinations even when only a subset is required for a run. Click
	the <strong>Select</strong> column header to clear the current selection. The table remains populated, but
	<strong>Total Cases</strong> changes to <code>0</code> and the configuration is marked as different from the
	current plan:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/deselect-all-cases.png", "Matrix and Cases configuration with all 24 generated cases cleared and Total Cases reduced to zero", "large") }}

<h3 id="choosing-individual-cases">Choosing individual cases</h3>
<p>Select the individual rows to include in the next run. In this example, cases 3 and 6 are selected, reducing
	the execution scope to two cases while preserving all 24 generated rows:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/selecting-two-cases.png", "Matrix and Cases configuration with only cases 3 and 6 selected from the 24 generated combinations", "large") }}

<h3 id="updating-the-case-plan">Updating the case plan</h3>
<p>Click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> to make the two-case selection the planned scope. The
	selection and <strong>Total Cases: 2</strong> indicator turn blue. The earlier 24-case run receives a
	<strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong> badge because its executed case set no longer matches the
	new plan. Because that retained run is excluded from the new plan's aggregate statistics, the test shows zero
	completed and passing cases:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/two-cases-after-update-plan.png", "Matrix and Cases after updating the plan to cases 3 and 6, with Total Cases and Plan set to two, the retained 24-case run marked as a plan mismatch, and aggregate statistics at zero", "large") }}

<h3 id="running-the-selected-cases">Running the selected cases</h3>
<p>Click <strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm the run. Orcraft executes only cases 3 and 6, and
	the completed test reports <strong>Plan: 2</strong>, <strong>Done: 2</strong>, <strong>Pass: 2</strong>, and
	<strong>Pass %: 100%</strong>. The log lists both selected matrix combinations, while the older 24-case run
	remains marked with <strong class="text-warning">Plan Mismatch</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/two-cases-run.png", "Completed Matrix and Cases run with planned cases 3 and 6 passing, aggregate Plan, Done, and Pass counts at two, and the older 24-case run retained as a plan mismatch", "large") }}

<h3 id="inspecting-the-selected-cases">Inspecting the selected cases</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed run. In the case table, cases 3 and 6 are the only rows marked
	as both <strong>Planned</strong> and <strong>Run</strong>. The results tree contains those same two cases, each
	with one passing <strong>Case Cycle 1</strong>, and the summary reports two planned, completed, and passing
	executions:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/two-case-run-details.png", "Matrix and Cases Details view showing cases 3 and 6 as the only planned and executed cases, each with one passing case cycle, and two completed passing executions", "large") }}
<p>Case selection changes the execution scope, not the matrix definition. Unselected combinations remain
	available in the table and can be restored without changing the test source.</p>